{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1200 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6d41f0b27387f5f7317fba8834049bafea0e24c7bf92035bd69bd277f24e6e4a" :edb/db-before-t 1199 :edb/db-after-t 1200 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534512 50 #inst "2026-10-07T19:13:50.422Z" 13194139534512 true] [17592186078154 1100 "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2$. Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$." 13194139534512 false] [17592186078154 1100 "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2.$ Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186078154 1145 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x - (-2)) \\\\\n&= 0 + 2(x + 2) \\\\\n&= 2(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8$. We get\n\n$$\nf(-1.8) \\mid \\approx L(-1.8); = 2(-1.8 + 2); = 2(0.2); = 0.4\n$$" 13194139534512 false] [17592186078154 1145 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =0+2(x+2) \\\\  & =2(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.8) & ≈L(-1.8) \\\\  & =2(-1.8+2) \\\\  & =2(0.2) \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186078155 1100 "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3$. Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4$, estimate the value of $g(3.6)$." 13194139534512 false] [17592186078155 1100 "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4,$ estimate the value of $g(3.6).$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186078155 1145 "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= g(a) + g^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= g(4) + g^{′}(4)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3$, and since\n\n$$\ng'(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ng'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 3 + 2(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6$. We get\n\n$$\ng(3.6) \\mid \\approx L(3.6); = 3 + 2(3.6 - 4); = 3 + 2(-0.4); = 3 - 0.8; = 2.2\n$$" 13194139534512 false] [17592186078155 1145 "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =g(a)+{g}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =g(4)+{g}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3,$ and since $g'(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ then $g'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+2(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3.6) & ≈L(3.6) \\\\  & =3+2(3.6-4) \\\\  & =3+2(-0.4) \\\\  & =3-0.8 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186078159 1100 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$. If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139534512 false] [17592186078159 1100 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0).$ If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139534512 true] [17592186078159 1145 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x - (-2)) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,-5)$, so we have\n\n$$\nf(-2)=-5\n$$\n\nAlso, we're told that $f'(-2)=3$, so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= -5 + 3(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.3$. We get\n\n$$\nf(-2.3) \\mid \\approx L(-2.3); =-5 + 3(-2.3 + 2); =-5 + 3(-0.3); =-5 - 0.9; =-5.9\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$, the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-2$, and therefore we have an overestimate." 13194139534512 false] [17592186078159 1145 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,-5),$ so we have $f(-2)=-5.$ Also, we're told that $f'(-2)=3,$ so we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-5+3(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.3) & ≈L(-2.3) \\\\  & =-5+3(-2.3+2) \\\\  & =-5+3(-0.3) \\\\  & =-5-0.9 \\\\  & =-5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$, the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-2,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." 13194139534512 true] [17592186078483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292627 13194139534512 true] [17592186078483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292635 13194139534512 true] [17592186078483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292639 13194139534512 true] [17592186078483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292642 13194139534512 true] [17592186078483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292645 13194139534512 true] [17592186078484 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292620 13194139534512 true] [17592186078484 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292631 13194139534512 true] [17592186078484 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292664 13194139534512 true] [17592186078484 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292678 13194139534512 true] [17592186078485 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292606 13194139534512 true] [17592186078485 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292613 13194139534512 true] [17592186078485 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292648 13194139534512 true] [17592186078485 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292671 13194139534512 true] [17592186078885 1017 #edb/ref 17592186076521 13194139534512 false] [17592186078885 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292658 13194139534512 true] [17592186078885 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534512 true] [17592186078885 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ passes through $(2, 3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0, 3)$. If $f^{′}(2) =-1$, estimate the value of $f(2.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139534512 false] [17592186078885 1100 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(2,3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0,3).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=-1,$ estimate the value of $f(2.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139534512 true] [17592186078885 1145 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(2,3),$ so we have $f(2)=3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(2)=-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+(-1)(x-2) \\\\  & =3-(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.2) & ≈L(2.2) \\\\  & =3-(2.2-2) \\\\  & =3-0.2 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(0,3),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an underestimate." 13194139534512 true] [17592186078893 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534512 true] [17592186078893 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nLet $f$ be a function such that $f^{′}(x) = x^{2} - 4$ and $f(4) = 13$. Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.5)$." 13194139534512 false] [17592186078893 1100 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-4$ and $f(4)=13.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.5).$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186078893 1145 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(4)=13,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-4,$ then ${f}^{′}(4)={4}^{2}-4=12.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=13+12(x-4).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3.5)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.5) & ≈L(3.5) \\\\  & =13+12(3.5-4) \\\\  & =13+12(-0.5) \\\\  & =13-6 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292606 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292607 13194139534512 true] [17592186292606 1089 "q-115261" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292606 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534512 true] [17592186292606 1100 "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292606 1113 #uuid "1aea0f50-2894-540a-8561-3e930ff1c7d7" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292606 1145 "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292608 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292609 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292610 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292611 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292612 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1041 "selection" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292608 13194139534512 true] [17592186292607 1110 #uuid "54487fe0-4811-5ed7-b1f8-8bc9f86a1dab" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292608 1034 "$f(x)$ is concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292608 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292608 1111 #uuid "b868e21c-5356-55f5-8804-a2e6df204c20" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292609 1034 "$f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292609 1111 #uuid "bbf57383-25d2-52e5-837b-7dd860dff200" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292610 1034 "$f(x)$ has a global minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292610 1111 #uuid "92744a5f-f08c-58db-9382-8ed6b2c3210c" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292611 1034 "$f(x)$ is concave up in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292611 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292611 1111 #uuid "156a96f0-7771-57d8-b692-2fc348552bcb" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292612 1034 "$f(x)$ is a linear function" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292612 1111 #uuid "60b8f4aa-96c3-59fc-92af-6f6571d80dc1" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292613 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292614 13194139534512 true] [17592186292613 1089 "q-115269" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292613 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534512 true] [17592186292613 1100 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-3,-4)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2).$ If ${f}^{′}(-3)=3,$ estimate the value of $f(-2.9)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-3$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139534512 true] [17592186292613 1113 #uuid "e38e2288-7689-5ff9-a85e-e9b1eee01cc5" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292613 1145 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-3)+{f}^{′}(-3)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(-3,-4),$ so we have $f(-3)=-4.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(-3)=3,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-4+3(x+3) \\\\  & =-4+3x+9 \\\\  & =3x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.9)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.9.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.9) & ≈L(-2.9) \\\\  & =3(-2.9)+5 \\\\  & =-8.7+5 \\\\  & =-3.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-3,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292615 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292616 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292617 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292618 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292619 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1041 "selection" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292617 13194139534512 true] [17592186292614 1110 #uuid "dacc59cc-937d-5a09-b84a-f5801d87553a" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292615 1034 "$f(-2.9)≈-4.6,$ an underestimate" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292615 1111 #uuid "024a12f6-59f4-5bd3-b474-7bd6e2654b6c" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292616 1034 "$f(-2.9)≈-4.3,$ an underestimate" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292616 1111 #uuid "8fa905b1-8323-533a-9b78-678ebb482923" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292617 1034 "$f(-2.9)≈-3.7,$ an overestimate" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292617 1111 #uuid "3cb82779-2d7a-50e5-b7e5-a6f21384b6ee" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292618 1034 "$f(-2.9)≈-3.7,$ an underestimate" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292618 1111 #uuid "02e0caa3-45a7-5dcf-bede-baaf50efb8f2" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292619 1034 "$f(-2.9)≈-4.3,$ an overestimate" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534512 true] [17592186292619 1111 #uuid "7bca371b-8aae-573a-b7ae-7acb84677d7e" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292620 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292621 13194139534512 true] [17592186292620 1089 "q-115608" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292620 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534512 true] [17592186292620 1100 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(4x-9)$ and $f(1)=-2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(1.5).$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292620 1113 #uuid "6a4c269f-3735-5033-b5a4-053c4a35fad1" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292620 1145 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=-2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(4x-9),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)={1}^{2}(4\\cdot 1-9)=-5.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-2-5(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.5)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.5) & ≈L(1.5) \\\\  & =-2-5(1.5-1) \\\\  & =-2-5(0.5) \\\\  & =-2-2.5 \\\\  & =-4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292622 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292623 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292624 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292625 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292626 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1041 "selection" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292624 13194139534512 true] [17592186292621 1110 #uuid "61d634dc-ad34-5150-80cb-4c224420b4f3" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292622 1034 "10" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534512 true] [17592186292622 1111 #uuid "3a410dcd-4bc7-5e3c-909e-48d27f273e26" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292623 1034 "12.5" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534512 true] [17592186292623 1111 #uuid "10ee60cb-df30-5049-a0a0-0ba248a66eb2" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292624 1034 "-4.5" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534512 true] [17592186292624 1111 #uuid "b94f95e5-8142-55ce-9622-a3813734ae16" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292625 1034 "-12" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534512 true] [17592186292625 1111 #uuid "ca4dbf67-af54-56b7-b10e-5898b7a4a000" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292626 1034 "-14.5" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534512 true] [17592186292626 1111 #uuid "ddb6b211-0b35-50b1-ac58-7255af910024" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292627 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292628 13194139534512 true] [17592186292627 1089 "q-314895" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292627 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534512 true] [17592186292627 1100 "An environmental researcher is tracking the depth $d$, in meters, of a lake during a dry season. The following table shows the rate at which the depth is decreasing.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(weeks)}$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${d}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{(meters/week)}$ | $-0.3$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.8$ | $-1.0$ |\n\nAt $t=2,$ the lake depth was $12$ meters. Use the tangent line approximation of $d$ at time $t=2$ to estimate the depth of the lake at $t=2.4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." 13194139534512 true] [17592186292627 1113 #uuid "a851630a-e575-5647-a3d4-704a604c24b5" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292627 1145 "The tangent line approximation of $d(t)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =d(a)+{d}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =d(2)+{d}^{′}(2)(t-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $d(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(2.4-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(0.4) \\\\  & =12-0.2 \\\\  & =11.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be $11.8$ meters." 13194139534512 true] [17592186292628 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292629 13194139534512 true] [17592186292628 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292630 13194139534512 true] [17592186292628 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534512 true] [17592186292628 1041 "field-1" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292628 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292629 13194139534512 true] [17592186292628 1110 #uuid "f3f19bba-2bca-54cb-9847-9655721fb279" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292629 1034 "11.8" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292629 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534512 true] [17592186292629 1111 #uuid "ae872bb6-20cb-51ac-b091-d6b7c3af38e7" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292630 1034 "12.2" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534512 true] [17592186292630 1111 #uuid "be37bf7e-ddbe-53a2-8a8b-9c225539a6b3" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292631 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292632 13194139534512 true] [17592186292631 1089 "q-314900" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292631 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534512 true] [17592186292631 1100 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292631 1113 #uuid "656c2c2e-203a-569b-a49f-ee98156b7c09" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292631 1145 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{\\pi }{5})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈L(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{30} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292633 13194139534512 true] [17592186292632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292634 13194139534512 true] [17592186292632 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534512 true] [17592186292632 1041 "field-1" 13194139534512 true] [17592186292632 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292633 131941